1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
Why does a soap bubble wear a rainbow

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:05,600
and why does it keep changing?

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:09,200
Its skin is a thin film of soapy water.

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:11,200
When white light hits it,

5
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:13,600
some reflects from the outer surface.

6
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,600
The rest enters the film,

7
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:17,600
bounces off the inner surface,

8
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,000
and emerges beside the first reflection.

9
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,200
One reflection flips phase by half a cycle.

10
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:24,800
The two waves overlap.

11
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:27,600
Depending on film thickness and viewing angle,

12
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:29,600
some wavelengths reinforce each other

13
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:30,800
while others cancel.

14
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:32,400
That is thin-film interference.

15
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,800
Gravity drains liquid down the bubble,

16
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:36,000
changing its thickness,

17
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,800
so the colours shift across its surface.

18
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:40,400
Just before it bursts,

19
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:42,800
extremely thin patches can look dark:

20
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:44,400
reflected visible wavelengths cancel.

21
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:47,200
The rainbow is made by light waves

22
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,400
adding—and subtracting.
